\chapter{BASIC Frontend Language}
This chapter describes the Dartmouth BASIC subset supported by the EightBol compiler's BASIC frontend. It covers every statement, function, operator, and control structure recognized by the lexer and parser, along with their inputs, outputs, processing, and side-effects.

\section{Lexical Elements}
The BASIC frontend tokenizes source code into the following categories:

\subsection{Keywords}
Keywords are reserved words that have special meaning in BASIC. They are case-insensitive.

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Keyword & Token \\
\hline
REM & :REM \\
LET & :LET \\
FOR & :FOR \\
TO & :TO \\
STEP & :STEP \\
NEXT & :NEXT \\
IF & :IF \\
THEN & :THEN \\
ELSE & :ELSE \\
GOTO & :GOTO \\
GOSUB & :GOSUB \\
RETURN & :RETURN \\
LOG & :LOG \\
FAULT & :FAULT \\
STOP & :STOP \\
METHOD & :METHOD \\
ON & :ON \\
CLASS & :CLASS \\
PROCEDURE & :PROCEDURE \\
END & :END \\
WHILE & :WHILE \\
UNTIL & :UNTIL \\
DO & :DO \\
LIBRARY & :LIBRARY \\
AND & :AND \\
OR & :OR \\
NOT & :NOT \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Operators}
Operators are symbols that perform arithmetic, logical, or relational operations.

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Operator & Description \\
\hline
+ & Addition \\
- & Subtraction \\
* & Multiplication \\
/ & Division \\
= & Equality or assignment (context-dependent) \\
< & Less than \\
> & Greater than \\
<= & Less than or equal \\
>= & Greater than or equal \\
<> & Not equal \\
. & Dot (used in qualified names) \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Delimiters}
These symbols separate tokens or have syntactic significance.

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Delimiter & Purpose \\
\hline
( & Left parenthesis \\
) & Right parenthesis \\
, & Comma (argument separator) \\
: & Colon (statement separator or label delimiter) \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Literals}
Literals represent constant values.

\subsubsection{Numeric Literals}
Numeric literals support multiple formats:

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Format & Example \\
\hline
Decimal & @code{42}, @code{1000} \\
Hexadecimal (&H prefix) & @code{&HFF}, @code{&h10} \\
Octal (&O prefix) & @code{&O77}, @code{&o10} \\
Binary (&B prefix) & @code{&B1010}, @code{&b11} \\
Dword (&D prefix, 32-bit) & @code{&D4294967295} \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

All numeric literal formats are converted to their decimal equivalent in the token stream.

\subsubsection{String Literals}
String literals are enclosed in double quotes (@code{"}).

Example: @code{"Hello, World!"}.

\subsection{Identifiers}
Identifiers name variables, procedures, methods, classes, and labels. They must begin with a letter and may contain letters, digits, underscores (@code{_}), and dollar signs (@code{$}). All identifiers are automatically normalized to PascalCase format in the AST. For example, @code{my_variable} becomes @code{MyVariable}, and @code{myVariable} becomes @code{MyVariable}.

\subsection{Line Numbers and Labels}
Each BASIC line may optionally begin with a line number (integer) followed by an optional quoted label (string) and a colon. Example:
@example
100 "Start" LET X = 5
@end example
Line numbers are used to generate @code{BASIC_Lnnnnnn} labels in the generated assembly. Quoted labels provide symbolic names for lines.

\section{Statements}
Each statement performs a specific action. Statements may be separated by colons on the same line.

\subsection{REM}
The @code{REM} statement (remark) inserts a comment that is ignored by the compiler. It has no effect on program execution.

\subsubsection{Inputs}
Any text following the @code{REM} keyword on the same line.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The lexer recognizes @code{REM} as a keyword and treats all text from @code{REM} to the end of the line as a comment. The apostrophe (@code{'}) is no longer recognized for comments; only the @code{REM} keyword and lines beginning with semicolon (@code{;}) are supported for comment handling.

\subsection{LET}
The @code{LET} statement assigns the value of an expression to a variable.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
LET variable = expression
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{variable}: An identifier naming the target variable.
- @code{expression}: An arithmetic or logical expression whose value is computed.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
The variable @code{variable} is updated with the result of @code{expression}.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser produces a move node that copies the value of @code{expression} into the storage location of @code{variable}.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects}
The variable's previous value is overwritten.

\subsection{INPUT}
Note: The @code{INPUT} statement is not implemented in this BASIC frontend.

\subsection{PRINT}
Note: The @code{PRINT} statement is not implemented in this BASIC frontend.

\subsection{IF...THEN...ELSE}
The @code{IF} statement conditionally executes one or two blocks of statements.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
IF condition THEN statement [ELSE statement]
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{condition}: An expression that evaluates to true (non-zero) or false (zero).
- @code{statement}: The statement to execute if @code{condition} is true.
- @code{else-stmt}: Optional statement to execute if @code{condition} is false.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None directly; execution flow is altered.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser builds an @code{if-node} with the condition, the @code{THEN} branch, and the optional @code{ELSE} branch. At runtime, the condition is evaluated; if true, the @code{THEN} branch is executed; otherwise, the @code{ELSE} branch (if present) is executed.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects}
Any side-effects occur within the executed branch.

\subsection{FOR...TO...STEP...NEXT}
The @code{FOR} statement creates a loop that iterates over a range of values.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
FOR variable = start TO end [STEP step]
   statement
NEXT [variable]
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{variable}: Identifier that serves as the loop counter.
- @code{start}: Initial value of the counter (expression).
- @code{end}: Terminal value; loop continues while counter has not passed this value (expression).
- @code{step}: Optional increment (default 1) added to @code{variable} each iteration (expression).
- @code{statement}: Body of the loop.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
The @code{variable} is updated each iteration.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser generates a @code{perform-node} representing a loop with:
- Varying variable: @code{variable}
- Initial value: @code{start}
- Increment: @code{step} (or 1 if omitted)
- Termination condition: counter > @code{end} (for positive step) or counter < @code{end} (for negative step). The current implementation uses a greater-than comparison regardless of step sign; see source for details.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects}
The loop variable is modified after each iteration. The loop body executes zero or more times.

\subsection{NEXT}
The @code{NEXT} statement marks the end of a @code{FOR} loop. The loop variable is optional.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
NEXT [variable]
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
Optional @code{variable}: If present, must match the corresponding @code{FOR} variable (though the current implementation ignores it).

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser produces a @code{goback-node} that jumps back to the loop header. The actual loop continuation logic resides in the @code{FOR} node.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
The loop variable is incremented by the step value before the condition is tested.

\subsection{WHILE}
The @code{WHILE} statement repeats a statement while a condition remains true.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
WHILE condition
   statement
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{condition}: Expression evaluated before each iteration.
- @code{statement}: Statement to execute if @code{condition} is true.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates an @code{if-node} where the @code{THEN} branch contains the statement and the @code{ELSE} branch is empty. The generated code effectively branches around the statement when the condition is false.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Determined by the enclosed statement.

\subsection{UNTIL}
The @code{UNTIL} statement repeats a statement until a condition becomes true (i.e., while the condition is false).

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
UNTIL condition
   statement
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{condition}: Expression evaluated before each iteration.
- @code{statement}: Statement to execute while @code{condition} is false.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing
The parser creates an @code{if-node} with the condition negated; the @code{THEN} branch holds the statement.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Determined by the enclosed statement.

\subsection{DO...NEXT WHILE / DO...NEXT UNTIL}
The @code{DO} statement executes a block of statements and then tests a condition to decide whether to repeat.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
DO
   statement-list
NEXT WHILE condition
@end example
or
@example
DO
   statement-list
NEXT UNTIL condition
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{statement-list}: Zero or more statements.
- @code{condition}: Expression tested after the block.

\subsubsection{Outputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser generates a @code{perform-node} labeled uniquely. For @code{WHILE}, the loop continues while @code{condition} is true; for @code{UNTIL}, it continues while @code{condition} is false (i.e., until @code{condition} becomes true). The condition is evaluated after the block.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Determined by the statements in the block.

\subsection{GOTO}
The @code{GOTO} statement transfers control unconditionally to a line number or label.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
GOTO target
@end example
where @code{target} is either a line number (identifier) or a quoted label (string).

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{target}: Line number or label to jump to.

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser produces a @code{goto-node} with the target. The backend resolves line numbers to @code{BASIC_Lnnnnnn} labels; quoted labels become symbolic labels.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Execution continues at the target line.

\subsection{GOSUB}
The @code{GOSUB} statement calls a subroutine, saving the return address for later @code{RETURN}.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
GOSUB target
@end example
where @code{target} can be:
- A line number (identifier)
- A quoted label (string)
- A string literal with @code{IN LIBRARY} (resident library call)
- A string literal with @code{ON expression} (method invocation on an object)
- A string literal with @code{SERVICE} (service-dispatch call)

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{target}: Subroutine destination as described above.

\subsubsection{Outputs
None; a return address is pushed onto the call stack implicitly by the generated code.

\subsubsection{Processing}
Depending on the variant, the parser produces:
- \textit{Local label or identifier}: a @code{perform-node} with the target.
- \textit{Library}: a @code{call-node} with library flag set.
- \textit{Method}: an @code{invoke-node} with object and method name.
- \textit{Service}: a @code{call-node} with service flag set.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
The return address is stored; normal subroutine call/return mechanics apply.

\subsection{RETURN}
The @code{RETURN} statement returns from the most recently called subroutine.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
RETURN
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
None.

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{goback-node} that jumps to the address saved by the matching @code{GOSUB}.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
The call stack is popped; execution resumes after the @code{GOSUB}.

\subsection{LOG FAULT}
The @code{LOG FAULT} statement logs a fault code for debugging.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
LOG FAULT "code"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{code}: A string literal containing the fault code (typically four digits).

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser builds a @code{log-fault-node} with the code.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Depending on the backend, a fault may be recorded or trigger a breakpoint.

\subsection{STOP}
The @code{STOP} statement halts program execution with an optional code.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
STOP "code"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{code}: String literal containing a stop code.

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{stop-run-node} with the code.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
The program terminates; control returns to the operating system or debugger.

\subsection{METHOD}
The @code{METHOD} statement defines a method within a class.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
METHOD "name" ON "class-name"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{name}: Method name (string).
- @code{class-name}: Name of the class to which the method belongs (string).

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{method-node} and sets the current class for subsequent definitions.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Establishes the current class context; later definitions belong to this class unless overridden.

\subsection{PROCEDURE
The @code{PROCEDURE} statement defines a standalone procedure.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
PROCEDURE "name"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{name}: Procedure name (string).

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{procedure-node}.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Makes the procedure available for calling via @code{GOSUB} or @code{CALL}.

\subsection{CLASS
The @code{CLASS} statement defines a class.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
CLASS "name"
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs}
- @code{name}: Class name (string).

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{program-node} for the class and sets @code{*current-class*} to the class name.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Subsequent @code{METHOD} and @code{PROCEDURE} definitions belong to this class until another @code{CLASS} or end of file.

\subsection{END
The @code{END} statement marks the end of the program.

\subsubsection{Syntax}
@example
END
@end example

\subsubsection{Inputs
None.

\subsubsection{Outputs
None.

\subsubsection{Processing}
The parser creates a @code{goback-node} that returns from the main program.

\subsubsection{Side-Effects
Execution stops; equivalent to @code{STOP} without a code in many backends.

### INVOKE

The `INVOKE` statement calls a method on an object using the OOPS dispatch mechanism.

#### Syntax
```
INVOKE object "method-name"
```

#### Inputs
- `object`: An identifier referencing an object instance.
- `"method-name"`: A string literal naming the method to invoke.

#### Outputs
None.

#### Processing
The parser creates an `invoke-node` with the object and method name.

#### Side-Effects
Depending on the backend, this emits a call to the virtual method table or a direct call to the method implementation.

### CALL

The `CALL` statement invokes a procedure or subroutine by name.

#### Syntax
```
CALL target
```

#### Inputs
- `target`: Identifier naming the procedure to call.

#### Outputs
None.

#### Processing
The parser creates a `call-node` with the target.

#### Side-Effects
Execution transfers to the procedure; a return address is pushed onto the stack.

### MOVE

The `MOVE` statement copies the value of an expression into a variable.

#### Syntax
```
MOVE expression TO variable
```

#### Inputs
- `expression`: Value to copy.
- `variable`: Identifier naming the destination variable.

#### Outputs
The variable `variable` receives the value of `expression`.

#### Processing
The parser creates a `move-node` with the source expression and destination identifier.

#### Side-Effects
The destination variable's previous value is overwritten.

### SET

The `SET` statement assigns the value of an expression to a variable (similar to `LET` but with different syntax).

#### Syntax
```
SET variable TO expression
```

#### Inputs
- `variable`: Identifier naming the target variable.
- `expression`: Value to assign.

#### Outputs
The variable `variable` is set to the value of `expression`.

#### Processing
The parser creates a `set-node` (functionally identical to a `move-node`).

#### Side-Effects
The variable's previous value is overwritten.

### ASSEMBLY ENTRY

The `ASSEMBLY ENTRY` statement inserts an assembly language label into the generated output.

#### Syntax
```
ASSEMBLY ENTRY "label"
```

#### Inputs
- `"label"`: String literal naming the assembly label.

#### Outputs
None.

#### Processing
The parser creates an `assembly-entry-node` with the label.

#### Side-Effects
The label is emitted in the assembly output, allowing manual code insertion or linking.

### COPY

The `COPY` statement includes the contents of another source file.

#### Syntax
```
COPY "filename"
```

#### Inputs
- `"filename"`: String literal naming the file to copy.

#### Outputs
None.

#### Processing
The parser creates a `copy-node` with the filename.

#### Side-Effects
At compile time, the contents of the named file are lexed and parsed as if they appeared at this point.

### Expression Statement

A standalone expression (without an enclosing statement) is permitted; its value is computed but discarded unless it has side-effects.

#### Syntax
```
expression
```

#### Inputs
- `expression`: Any valid arithmetic, logical, or relational expression.

#### Outputs
None (value discarded).

#### Processing
The parser treats the expression as a statement that evaluates the expression.

#### Side-Effects
Any side-effects inherent in the expression (e.g., function calls with side-effects) occur.

## Expressions

Expressions compute values using operators, operands, and parentheses.

### Arithmetic Operators

| Operator | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `+`      | Addition |
| `-`      | Subtraction |
| `*`      | Multiplication |
| `/`      | Division |

### Relational Operators

| Operator | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `=`      | Equality |
| `<>`     | Not equal |
| `<`      | Less than |
| `<=`     | Less than or equal |
| `>`      | Greater than |
| `>=`     | Greater than or equal |

### Logical Operators

| Operator | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `AND`    | Logical conjunction |
| `OR`     | Logical disjunction |
| `NOT`    | Logical negation |

### Operator Precedence (highest to lowest)
1. Parentheses
2. `NOT`
3. Multiplicative (`*`, `/`)
4. Additive (`+`, `-`)
5. Relational (`=`, `<>`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`)
6. `AND`
7. `OR`

### Primary Expressions

Primary expressions are the building blocks of more complex expressions.

#### Numeric Literals
Example: `42`

#### String Literals
Example: `"Hello"`

#### Identifiers
Example: `Count`, `Total$`, `_flag`

#### `SELF`
Refers to the current object instance within a method.

#### `NULL`
Represents the null object reference.

#### Parenthesized Expressions
`( expression )` groups subexpressions to override precedence.

### Reader Macro Analogies

This BASIC frontend supports multiple number literal formats similar to reader macros in Lisp:

- **Hexadecimal Numbers**: Prefix with @code{&H} or @code{&h}. Example: @code{&HFF} → 255, @code{&h10} → 16.
- **Octal Numbers**: Prefix with @code{&O} or @code{&o}. Example: @code{&O77} → 63, @code{&o10} → 8.
- **Binary Numbers**: Prefix with @code{&B} or @code{&b}. Example: @code{&B1010} → 10, @code{&b11} → 3.
- **Dword (32-bit) Numbers**: Prefix with @code{&D} or @code{&d}. Example: @code{&D4294967295} → 4294967295. Values must be in range 0 to 2^32-1.
- **Decimal Numbers**: Default format without prefix. Example: @code{42}, @code{1000}.

All numeric formats are converted to their decimal equivalent in the token stream for processing.

## Functions

The BASIC frontend does not provide intrinsic functions beyond those exposed via libraries or OOPS methods. Functions are invoked using `GOSUB` with appropriate targets (library, method, or service). Examples include:

- **Mathematical Functions**: Access via library calls (e.g., `GOSUB "SIN" IN LIBRARY`).
- **String Manipulation**: Similarly via library or object methods.
- **System Calls**: Through service-dispatch mechanisms.

Consult the specific library or class documentation for available functions.

## Control Structures Summary

| Structure   | Purpose |
|-------------|---------|
| `REM`       | Comment |
| `LET`/`SET`/`MOVE` | Assignment |
| `IF...THEN...ELSE` | Conditional branching |
| `FOR...TO...STEP...NEXT` | Counted loop |
| `WHILE`     | Conditional loop (pre-test) |
| `UNTIL`     | Conditional loop (pre-test with negation) |
| `DO...NEXT WHILE/UNTIL` | Conditional loop (post-test) |
| `GOTO`      | Unconditional jump |
| `GOSUB`/`RETURN` | Subroutine call and return |
| `LOG FAULT` | Debugging fault logging |
| `STOP`      | Program termination |
| `METHOD`/`PROCEDURE`/`CLASS` | OOPS definition |
| `END`       | Program end |
| `INVOKE`    | OOPS method invocation |
| `CALL`      | Procedure call |
| `ASSEMBLY ENTRY` | Inline assembly label |
| `COPY`      | File inclusion |

This chapter provides a complete reference for the BASIC frontend language as implemented in EightBol. For further details, consult the lexer and parser source files.

\section{Identifier Normalization}

All identifiers in BASIC programs are automatically normalized to PascalCase format during parsing, before AST node creation. This ensures consistent naming across the generated code.

\subsection{Normalization Rules}
\begin{itemize}
\item Underscores (@code{_}) are treated as word separators.
\item Each word is capitalized (first letter uppercase, remainder lowercase).
\item The normalized identifier is formed by concatenating all capitalized words without separators.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Examples}
\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
Original & Normalized \\
\hline
@code{my_variable} & @code{MyVariable} \\
@code{myVariable} & @code{MyVariable} \\
@code{MY_CONST} & @code{MyCon} (note: each segment capitalized) \\
@code{counter} & @code{Counter} \\
@code{total_sum_value} & @code{TotalSumValue} \\
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Effect on AST}
All identifiers appearing in the abstract syntax tree (AST) have been normalized. This applies to:
\begin{itemize}
\item Variable names in @code{LET}, @code{SET}, @code{MOVE} statements.
\item Loop variables in @code{FOR}/@code{NEXT}.
\item Procedure and method names.
\item Class names.
\item Labels and line number references.
\end{itemize}

This normalization ensures consistent identifier treatment throughout compilation and aids in cross-language and cross-platform consistency.
